\section{Relate an Instant to an Imperative Call}

\Cref{ch:ext-362} lowers one Lustre counter by hand. A compiler must justify the same kind of lowering for nested node instances and sampled clocks. Bourke and colleagues translate scheduled, normalized Lustre equations to an imperative object language, Obc, and then generate CompCert Clight \cite{bourke2017verifiedlustre}. A reset method initializes memory; a step method implements one logical instant. The interesting change is not the replacement of a stream identifier by a variable name. It is the change from equations constraining whole streams to statements that observe and modify one concrete memory.

\section{State the Relation at the Boundary of a Step}

Take a parent with two instances of a toy node. The node starts with delay \(0\), outputs its old delay, and then remembers the current input. The left instance is always active, while the right instance is sampled using an enable. A source history determines the latest present input of each instance independently, or the initial zero if no such input exists. The target memory relation says that each instance's delay field contains exactly that value at step entry. It also says that the two instances have distinct logical memory, even if they share one implementation of the step method.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cccccl}
\hline
Tick & Input & Right active & Left out & Right out & Memory after \\
\hline
0 & 7 & no & 0 & --- & \((7,0)\) \\
1 & 8 & yes & 7 & 0 & \((8,8)\) \\
2 & 9 & no & 8 & --- & \((9,8)\) \\
3 & 10 & yes & 9 & 8 & \((10,10)\) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The right output at tick 3 is \(8\), not \(9\) and not \(10\). If the target advances the right child unconditionally and merely masks the result, it returns \(9\). If it overwrites the right delay before reading it, it returns \(10\). \Cref{fig:visual-383} relates two successive step boundaries. This sample illustrates only a small instance of the simulation invariant; additional memory and clock conditions are needed for the full compiler.

