% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Check Initialization and Preservation Separately}

\begin{verbatim}
reset(parent):
    parent.left.saved := 0
    parent.right.saved := 0

child_step(child, input):
    old := child.saved
    child.saved := input
    return old

parent_step(parent, input, enable):
    left := child_step(parent.left, input)
    right := Absent
    if enable:
        right := Present(child_step(parent.right, input))
    return (Present(left), right)
\end{verbatim}

Initialization establishes the invariant for the empty history. For the induction step, read the old saved field to obtain the source delay value. On an active clock the write changes the saved field to the current input, establishing the invariant for the extended history. On an inactive clock neither a source sample nor an imperative write occurs for that child. Applying the two child arguments separately works because a write to one instance's state does not invalidate the invariant for the other.

\section{Keep the Proof Boundary When Optimizing}

Adjacent statements guarded by the same clock can be fused only when evaluating the guard once instead of twice preserves which statements run. In an imperative IR, for example, the first statement must not change the memory used by a later guard in a way that makes that second test differ. Likewise, scheduling current values before writes is not enough if a later computation still refers to a previous value that was overwritten. Keep explicit reads, writes, and clock facts until those obligations are discharged.

Test with unequal inputs, a long absent interval, and enable asserted at the first instant. Then insert a reset between two independent runs; the first active output after reset must be the initial value again. These fixtures catch likely implementation errors, but are not a replacement for the invariant or the verified connection between translation passes. The language-to-object proof and the object-to-Clight proof also have different boundaries: the former explains why step behavior denotes the source streams; the latter explains why the C representation implements those step methods.

\section{Justify an In-Place Update With Old-State Dependencies}

Consider a second node whose two delays exchange values at every active
instant. Its state begins at \((1,2)\). The first step must leave
\((2,1)\), and the second restores \((1,2)\). Sequential assignments
that read the fields directly, first assigning the left field and
then the right, leave \((2,2)\). The source's equations are not
commands that can be executed in their textual order.

\begin{verbatim}
exchange_step(state, active):
    if not active: return Absent
    left_before := state.left
    right_before := state.right
    state.left := right_before
    state.right := left_before
    return Present(left_before, right_before)
\end{verbatim}

An imperative compiler does not need to copy the whole node record.
For each scheduled statement, identify which values belong to the
entry memory and which were computed at this instant. If an old
value has a use after the proposed overwrite, stage that old value in
a local or reorder the write after all such uses where legal. The
optimization proof can relate the staged locals to old memory and the
updated fields to exit memory. The two need not agree halfway through
the procedure; the relation is reestablished when the step returns.

Use a trace with one inactive instant between exchanges. On that
instant the exit memory must equal the entry memory, and no output
tuple is published. Then attempt the same optimization with two
distinct child instances whose memory layout has been accidentally
coalesced. A verifier must reject the layout even if a run with
identical child inputs happens to produce the same outputs. The
instance separation condition supports all traces, not just those
where the memory values happen to coincide.
